# Electromagnetism
#
# The canonical home for the Maxwell equations, potentials and the Lorenz gauge, waves, transmission lines, and radiation, with circuits as the quasi-static limit that engineering builds on.

#>physics_em
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!physics
#:form #!curriculum
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Electromagnetism"
 ~#claim: "Four Maxwell equations and the Lorentz force describe every electric, magnetic, and optical effect outside quantum theory; lumped circuits are their limit when parts are small next to a wavelength."
 ~#proof: "Griffiths and Jackson for the field theory, Pozar for lines and matching, Jackson and Okun for the history of gauges and the Lorenz name."
 ~#door: "Follow the prerequisite chain from statics to waves, then measure the speed of light with a microwave oven."
 ~#audience: "physics and electrical engineering students in their junior year"
 continuity: "Electromagnetism | small next to a wavelength"
 cold_open: "Circuits are just Maxwell's equations when every wire is small next to a wavelength, and a microwave oven shows you that wavelength."
}

